request.prompt:
You are an independent evaluation judge. Grade the proposed remedy, not the
writing style of this prompt. A good remedy changes a concrete future behavior,
is broad enough to cover the failure mode without becoming a platitude, uses a
real `Use when ...` trigger when it is a skill, leaks no evidence-specific
project nouns into the remedy text, and uses the cheapest adequate remedy type.

Score every dimension from 1 (poor) to 5 (excellent). Give exactly one concise
sentence of justification for each score. Do not infer facts that are absent.

PROPOSAL:
{
  "confidence": 0.6,
  "dedup": [
    {
      "existing": "R1",
      "reason": "R1 (fake-verify-before-claim) addresses verifying completion claims before reporting done — an honesty/verification-of-result failure. These incidents are about the agent solving/delivering the wrong scope in the first place, a distinct request-comprehension failure, not a verification gap.",
      "verdict": "clear"
    },
    {
      "existing": "R2",
      "reason": "R2 (Fake Release Discipline) requires running a declared verification command before reporting a release complete — again a verification-before-claim rule. It has no bearing on whether the agent understood and matched the requested scope.",
      "verdict": "clear"
    },
    {
      "existing": "R3",
      "reason": "R3 concerns unauthorized deletion of content despite a stated constraint — a destructive-action failure. These incidents involve no deletion or constraint violation, only delivering a broader/different outcome than the narrow scope requested.",
      "verdict": "clear"
    },
    {
      "existing": "R4",
      "reason": "R4 concerns acting in the wrong branch/workspace — an environment-state failure. These incidents are about misjudging the scope of the deliverable itself, unrelated to which branch or workspace the work happened in.",
      "verdict": "clear"
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "incident_id": 9,
      "quote": "That's not what I asked; the request was a one-file note."
    },
    {
      "incident_id": 19,
      "quote": "You solved a different problem; I asked for the export only."
    }
  ],
  "failure_statement": "When a user's request specifies a narrow, bounded deliverable, the agent sometimes delivers a different or broader outcome than requested, forcing the user to explicitly point out that the wrong problem was solved.",
  "remedy_content": {
    "target": "global",
    "text": "## Scope Discipline\n\nWhen a request names a narrow or bounded deliverable (e.g. \"just the export\", \"a one-file note\"), produce exactly that — no extra files, demos, or unrequested additions. If you believe broader scope is genuinely needed, ask before expanding rather than delivering something different and explaining afterward."
  },
  "remedy_type": "claude-md",
  "routing_rationale": "Both promoted incidents (cinder #9, harbor #19) show the same shape: the user requested a narrowly-scoped, bounded deliverable (\"a one-file note\", \"the export only\") and the agent instead produced something broader or different, requiring the user to explicitly correct scope. This is a request-comprehension/scoping failure, not a verification-of-completion failure (R1, R2) and not a destructive-action or wrong-environment failure (R3, R4) — so it is clear of all four existing remedies and needs a new one. It is not mechanically enforceable via a hook (no tool call or bash pattern reliably detects \"scope drift\" from a semantic reading of the request), and it does not require multi-step procedural guidance that would justify a skill's context cost. A short standing rule that instructs the agent to mirror the requested scope exactly (or ask before expanding it) is the cheapest fix that addresses the root cause, so per the preference order this routes to claude-md. Given both incidents come from different projects (cinder, harbor) with no project-specific hook, this is a general behavioral norm and belongs in the global CLAUDE.md rather than a per-project file.",
  "singleton": false
}
